23
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:59,490
And I think the fundamental
question that I hope to get at

24
00:00:59,490 --> 00:01:09,565
is, why do language
models work so well?

25
00:01:09,565 --> 00:01:12,370

26
00:01:12,370 --> 00:01:14,830
And one thing that
I encourage everyone

27
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:17,050
to do that I found to be
extremely helpful in trying

28
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:21,535
to answer this question
is to use a tool which

29
00:01:21,535 --> 00:01:27,085
is manually inspect data.

30
00:01:27,085 --> 00:01:29,950

31
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:31,600
And I'll give a short anecdote.

32
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,330
I've been doing this
for a long time.

33
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:38,282
So in 2019, I was trying to
build one of the first lung

34
00:01:38,282 --> 00:01:40,240
cancer classifiers, so
there would be an image,

35
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,490
and you have to say like,
OK, what type of lung cancer

36
00:01:42,490 --> 00:01:43,600
is this?

37
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,020
And my first thing
was like, OK, if I

38
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:49,458
want to train a neural
network to do this,

39
00:01:49,458 --> 00:01:51,250
I should be able to,
at least, do the task.

40
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:52,810
So I went to my
advisor and I said

41
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:55,270
like, oh, I want to learn
to do this task first.

42
00:01:55,270 --> 00:01:58,330
And he said, Jason, you
need a medical degree

43
00:01:58,330 --> 00:01:59,770
and like three
years of pathology

44
00:01:59,770 --> 00:02:01,780
experience to even do this task.

45
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:03,730
And I found that a
bit discouraging,

46
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:05,560
but I went and did it anyways.

47
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:09,130
So I basically looked at the
specific type of lung cancer

48
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:10,449
that I was working on.

49
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:13,810
And I'd read all the papers on
how to classify different types.

50
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:15,820
And I went to pathologists
and I said, OK, I

51
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:17,860
try to classify these,
what do I do wrong?

52
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:20,080
And then, what do
you think of that?

53
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:24,040
And in the end, I learned how to
do this task of classifying lung

54
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:25,150
cancer.

55
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:28,330
And the result of this
was, I gained intuitions

56
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:32,590
about the task that
led to many papers.

57
00:02:32,590 --> 00:02:33,190
OK.

58
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:38,560
So first, I will do a quick
review of language models.

59
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:43,060
So the way language models are
trained are with the next word

60
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:44,500
prediction task.

61
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:51,260
So let's say you
have a sentence,

62
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:55,460
Dartmouth students like to--

63
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:57,500
and the goal of
next word prediction

64
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:59,420
is, you have some
words that come before,

65
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:01,790
and then you want to
predict the next word.

66
00:03:01,790 --> 00:03:05,600
And what the language
model does is

67
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:08,780
it outputs a probability
for every single word

68
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:09,800
in the vocabulary.

69
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:21,050
So vocabulary would be like,
a aardvark, drink, study,

70
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:24,800
and then all the
way to zucchini.

71
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,690
And then the language
model is going

72
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:29,500
to put a probability over
every single word here.

73
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,150
So the probability of
a being the next word

74
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:34,490
is something really small.

75
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:37,310
Aardvark is something
really small.

76
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:43,790
And then maybe drink is
like say, 0.6, study is 0.3,

77
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:45,440
zucchini is, again,
really small.

78
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:49,210

79
00:03:49,210 --> 00:03:53,050
And then the way that you
train the language model is

80
00:03:53,050 --> 00:03:58,920
you say, I want-- let's say
drink is the correct word here,

81
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:03,660
I want this number here, 0.6,
to be as close as possible to 1.

82
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:09,090
So your loss is basically
how close is the actual word?

83
00:04:09,090 --> 00:04:11,430
The probability of
the actual next word?

84
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:13,980
And you want this loss
to be as low as possible.

85
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:16,769

86
00:04:16,769 --> 00:04:17,269
OK.

87
00:04:17,269 --> 00:04:29,700
So the first intuition that I
would encourage everyone to use

88
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:52,900
is, next word prediction is,
massively multi-task learning.

89
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:54,963
And what I mean by
this is the following,

90
00:04:54,963 --> 00:04:56,005
I'll give a few examples.

91
00:04:56,005 --> 00:04:59,870

92
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:03,700
So when you train
a language model

93
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:07,870
on a large enough database,
a large enough data set

94
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:10,270
on this task of next
word prediction,

95
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:12,560
you have a lot of sentences
that you can learn from.

96
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:16,660
So for example, there might be
some sentence, in my free time

97
00:05:16,660 --> 00:05:18,760
I like to-- and
the language model

98
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:21,580
has to learn that code should be
higher probability than the word

99
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:25,480
banana, so it
learns some grammar.

100
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:29,080
It will learn lexical
semantics, so somewhere

101
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:32,200
in your data set there might be
a sentence, I went to the store

102
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:35,463
to buy papaya,
dragonfruit, and durian.

103
00:05:35,463 --> 00:05:36,880
And the language
model should know

104
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:41,080
that the probability of durian
should be higher than squirrel.

105
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:43,490
The language model will
learn world knowledge.

106
00:05:43,490 --> 00:05:45,865
So there will be some sentence
on the internet that says,

107
00:05:45,865 --> 00:05:48,193
the capital of
Azerbaijan is-- and then

108
00:05:48,193 --> 00:05:50,110
the language model should
learn that it should

109
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:53,500
be Baku instead of London.

110
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,720
You can learn traditional NLP
tasks like sentiment analysis.

111
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:58,683
So there will be
some sentence, I

112
00:05:58,683 --> 00:06:00,100
was engaged on the
edge of my seat

113
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:01,880
the whole time the movie was.

114
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:03,520
And the language
model looks like, OK,

115
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:05,260
if these are the
prior next words,

116
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:10,110
the next word should
probably be good and not bad.

117
00:06:10,110 --> 00:06:14,060
And then finally-- or another
example is translation.

118
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:16,580
So here, you might
see some sentence.

119
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:19,040
The word for pretty
and Spanish is--

120
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:21,140
and then the
language model should

121
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:24,920
weigh bonita more than ola.

122
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:25,970
Spatial reasoning.

123
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:27,620
So you might even
have some sentence

124
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:30,290
like, Iroh went to the
kitchen to make some tea.

125
00:06:30,290 --> 00:06:33,440
Standing next to Iroh,
Zuko pondered his destiny.

126
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:36,020
Zuko left the-- and
then kitchen should be

127
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:38,860
higher probability than store.

128
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:41,240
And then finally, even
some math questions.

129
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:45,460
So you might have some
arithmetic exam answer key

130
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:47,710
somewhere on the internet,
and then the language model

131
00:06:47,710 --> 00:06:50,085
looks at this and says, OK,
the next word should probably

132
00:06:50,085 --> 00:06:53,120
be 15 and not 11.

133
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:55,760
And you can have basically
millions of tasks like this

134
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,550
when you have a huge data set.

135
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:01,910
And you can think of this as
basically extreme multitask

136
00:07:01,910 --> 00:07:03,720
learning.

137
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:07,920
And these are sort of very
clean examples of tasks,

138
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:17,000
but I'll give an example of how
arbitrary some of these tasks

139
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:17,540
can be.

140
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:21,430

141
00:07:21,430 --> 00:07:26,530
So here's a sentence
from Wikipedia.

142
00:07:26,530 --> 00:07:34,560
Biden married Neilia.

143
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:37,530
OK, and then now, pretend
you're the language model.

144
00:07:37,530 --> 00:07:41,310
And you could say, OK,
what's the next word here?

145
00:07:41,310 --> 00:07:43,875
And the next word
here is hunter.

146
00:07:43,875 --> 00:07:46,657
So it's Biden's
first wife, and so,

147
00:07:46,657 --> 00:07:48,240
OK, what's the
language model learning

148
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:50,010
to predict from
predicting this word,

149
00:07:50,010 --> 00:07:51,960
I guess like world knowledge.

150
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:55,680

151
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:58,660
And then what's the
next word after this?

152
00:07:58,660 --> 00:08:00,510
It turns out the
next word is a comma.

153
00:08:00,510 --> 00:08:06,700
So here the model is learning,
basically, comma prediction.

154
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:09,760
And then what's the
next word after that?

155
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:13,350
I think it's kind of hard to
know, but the answer is a.

156
00:08:13,350 --> 00:08:15,700
And I guess this is
like maybe grammar,

157
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:17,230
but like somewhat arbitrary.

158
00:08:17,230 --> 00:08:20,320

159
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:23,120
And then what's the
next word after that?

160
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:24,585
It turns out it's student.

161
00:08:24,585 --> 00:08:27,760

162
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:30,340
And this I would
say, I don't know

163
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:35,110
what task this is, this is
it could have been woman,

164
00:08:35,110 --> 00:08:36,850
it could have been
something else,

165
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:39,760
so this is like a
pretty arbitrary task.

166
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:41,770
And the point that I'm
trying to make here

167
00:08:41,770 --> 00:08:43,539
is that, the next
word prediction

168
00:08:43,539 --> 00:08:45,400
task is really challenging.

169
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:47,590
So if you do this over
the entire database

170
00:08:47,590 --> 00:08:51,085
you're going to
learn a lot of tasks.

171
00:08:51,085 --> 00:08:51,585
OK.

172
00:08:51,585 --> 00:08:58,480

173
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:01,210
The next intuition
I want to talk about

174
00:09:01,210 --> 00:09:15,720
is scaling, which is, by the
way, say scaling compute.

175
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,080
And by the way, compute
is equal to how much data

176
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:21,330
you have times the size
of the language model.

177
00:09:21,330 --> 00:09:26,600

178
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:28,430
Reliably improves loss.

179
00:09:28,430 --> 00:09:36,770

180
00:09:36,770 --> 00:09:40,580
And this idea was
basically pioneered

181
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:47,060
by Kaplan et Al in 2020.

182
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:49,340
I would encourage you
guys to read the paper.

183
00:09:49,340 --> 00:09:53,930
And what this basically says
is, you can have a plot here,

184
00:09:53,930 --> 00:09:56,120
and we'll see many
plots like this,

185
00:09:56,120 --> 00:10:05,600
where the x-axis is compute,
and the y-axis is loss.

186
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:10,100
And what this intuition says
is, you can train one language

187
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:11,660
model, you'll have that loss.

188
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:13,550
And obviously, you
want loss to be lower,

189
00:10:13,550 --> 00:10:16,340
so you can train the next
one, it'll have that loss.

190
00:10:16,340 --> 00:10:18,950
If you train the one after
that, it'll have that loss,

191
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:22,400
then if you train the one after
that, we'll have that loss.

192
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:26,270
And you can basically predict
the loss of a language model

193
00:10:26,270 --> 00:10:29,790
based on how much compute
you're going to use to train it.

194
00:10:29,790 --> 00:10:32,310
And the reason why
this is called a law

195
00:10:32,310 --> 00:10:36,390
is that in this paper they
showed that the x-axis here

196
00:10:36,390 --> 00:10:39,030
is actually seven
orders of magnitude.

197
00:10:39,030 --> 00:10:41,310
So basically, it
would be surprising

198
00:10:41,310 --> 00:10:44,460
if the trend broke
if you continued.

199
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:46,950
And the important
thing about this

200
00:10:46,950 --> 00:10:49,920
is that the line does
not go like that,

201
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:54,990
because if it went like
that, then it would saturate,

202
00:10:54,990 --> 00:10:58,080
and then putting more compute or
training a larger language model

203
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:00,360
wouldn't actually
lead to lower loss.

204
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:13,630

205
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:18,670
OK, so I think a question that
we don't have a good answer

206
00:11:18,670 --> 00:11:23,620
to as a field, but I'll give
you like a handwavy answer is,

207
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:26,710
why does scaling up the
size of your language model

208
00:11:26,710 --> 00:11:28,840
improve the loss?

209
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:31,300
And I'll give like two
basically hand-wavy answers.

210
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:39,160
So here's a small LM,
and here's large LM.

211
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:42,250
So one thing that's important
is, how good is your language

212
00:11:42,250 --> 00:11:44,725
model at memorizing facts?

213
00:11:44,725 --> 00:11:48,610

214
00:11:48,610 --> 00:11:50,770
And imagine you're a
small language model,

215
00:11:50,770 --> 00:11:53,410
and you see a bunch of
facts on the internet.

216
00:11:53,410 --> 00:11:56,230
You have to be pretty
choosy in which facts

217
00:11:56,230 --> 00:11:59,110
you memorize because if you
don't have that many parameters,

218
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:01,570
you're like, oh, I can only
memorize a million facts,

219
00:12:01,570 --> 00:12:03,070
is this one of the
facts that I want

220
00:12:03,070 --> 00:12:04,840
to memorize at the lowest loss?

221
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:07,450
And so you have to
be very selective.

222
00:12:07,450 --> 00:12:12,800
Whereas if you're a
large language model,

223
00:12:12,800 --> 00:12:15,470
you can memorize a
lot of tail knowledge.

224
00:12:15,470 --> 00:12:18,230
And so basically, every fact
you see, you don't have to say,

225
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:19,772
is this something
I want to memorize?

226
00:12:19,772 --> 00:12:21,840
You can just memorize it.

227
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:25,170
And then the other
hand-wavy answer I'll give

228
00:12:25,170 --> 00:12:31,740
is small language models tend
to learn first order heuristics.

229
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:35,310
So if you're like a
small language model,

230
00:12:35,310 --> 00:12:37,818
you're like already struggling
to get the grammar correct,

231
00:12:37,818 --> 00:12:40,110
you're not going to do your
best to try to get the math

232
00:12:40,110 --> 00:12:42,320
problem exactly correct.

233
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:46,700
Whereas if you're a
large language model,

234
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:50,090
you have a lot of parameters
in your forward pass,

235
00:12:50,090 --> 00:12:52,190
and you can try to do
really complicated things

236
00:12:52,190 --> 00:12:55,318
to get the next token correct,
and to get the loss as

237
00:12:55,318 --> 00:12:55,985
low as possible.

238
00:12:55,985 --> 00:13:08,110

239
00:13:08,110 --> 00:13:10,750
OK, so the third
intuition I'll talk about

240
00:13:10,750 --> 00:13:26,590
is, while overall,
loss improves smoothly,

241
00:13:26,590 --> 00:13:36,875
individual tasks can
improve suddenly.

242
00:13:36,875 --> 00:13:41,530

243
00:13:41,530 --> 00:13:43,780
And here's what I mean by this.

244
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:47,350
So you can write
your overall loss.

245
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:53,980
By this I mean, if you
take some corpus of data

246
00:13:53,980 --> 00:13:57,910
and you compute the overall loss
on every word in that data set,

247
00:13:57,910 --> 00:14:02,320
the overall loss because we know
that next word prediction is

248
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:04,060
massively multitask
learning, you

249
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:06,970
can decompose this
overall loss into the loss

250
00:14:06,970 --> 00:14:08,620
of every single individual task.

251
00:14:08,620 --> 00:14:11,650
So you have, I don't
know, some small number

252
00:14:11,650 --> 00:14:20,570
times the loss of say grammar,
plus some small number,

253
00:14:20,570 --> 00:14:26,400
times the loss of like
sentiment analysis,

254
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:36,150
plus some small number, times
the loss of world knowledge,

255
00:14:36,150 --> 00:14:36,960
plus--

256
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:42,090
and then all the way to, let's
say you have something times

257
00:14:42,090 --> 00:14:47,190
the loss of math.

258
00:14:47,190 --> 00:14:49,830
And so you can basically
write your overall loss

259
00:14:49,830 --> 00:14:53,020
as the weighted sum of the
individual tasks in the data

260
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:54,370
set.

261
00:14:54,370 --> 00:14:56,860
And now the question
is, let's say

262
00:14:56,860 --> 00:15:01,530
I improve my loss
from four to three,

263
00:15:01,530 --> 00:15:06,337
do each of these individual
tasks improve at the same rate?

264
00:15:06,337 --> 00:15:07,670
Well, I would say, probably not.

265
00:15:07,670 --> 00:15:11,390
So if you have a good
enough language model,

266
00:15:11,390 --> 00:15:13,130
it's already doing
basically, perfectly

267
00:15:13,130 --> 00:15:15,210
on grammar and
sentiment analysis,

268
00:15:15,210 --> 00:15:16,580
so this might not improve.

269
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:19,170

270
00:15:19,170 --> 00:15:21,330
So this might be saturated.

271
00:15:21,330 --> 00:15:25,180

272
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:29,230
And maybe, the law-- the laws
on math is not saturated,

273
00:15:29,230 --> 00:15:32,005
it's not that good at math yet,
so this could improve suddenly.

274
00:15:32,005 --> 00:15:37,850

275
00:15:37,850 --> 00:15:40,325
And I'll redraw the
diagram to show this.

276
00:15:40,325 --> 00:15:46,840

277
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:55,270
So again, compute
here, and loss here,

278
00:15:55,270 --> 00:15:57,730
and your overall
loss is like this.

279
00:15:57,730 --> 00:16:01,290

280
00:16:01,290 --> 00:16:05,710
What I'm saying
is, there might be

281
00:16:05,710 --> 00:16:09,230
some part of that overall
loss that scales like this.

282
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:13,030
So this is say, grammar.

283
00:16:13,030 --> 00:16:19,060
So for example, you could say,
if GPT-3.5 is there and GPT-4 is

284
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:22,810
there, you haven't actually
improved the grammar that much.

285
00:16:22,810 --> 00:16:26,640
On the other hand, you might
have something like this,

286
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:30,210
for say, doing math
or a harder task,

287
00:16:30,210 --> 00:16:34,680
where the difference between
GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 will be much

288
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,210
larger.

289
00:16:37,210 --> 00:16:49,880
And it turns out you can look at
a big set of tasks, which I did,

290
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:54,040
and you can look at, what's the
shape of these scaling curves?

291
00:16:54,040 --> 00:16:58,630
So I looked at 202 tasks.

292
00:16:58,630 --> 00:17:01,870
There's this corpus called
Big Bench which has 200 tasks.

293
00:17:01,870 --> 00:17:05,859
I looked at all of them and
here's the distribution.

294
00:17:05,859 --> 00:17:17,200
So you have-- this was 29%
of tasks that were smooth.

295
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:23,109
So if I draw the scaling plot,
compute is on the x-axis,

296
00:17:23,109 --> 00:17:25,599
and then here we have
accuracy instead of loss,

297
00:17:25,599 --> 00:17:30,930
so higher is better, then
you have something like this.

298
00:17:30,930 --> 00:17:36,570
I believe this was
22% will be flat.

299
00:17:36,570 --> 00:17:39,690
So if you have your scaling
curve, it'll just all be 0,

300
00:17:39,690 --> 00:17:42,540
the task was too hard.

301
00:17:42,540 --> 00:17:46,230
2% will be something
called inverse scaling,

302
00:17:46,230 --> 00:17:48,880
I'll talk about this in a sec.

303
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:52,570
But what that means is
the accuracy actually

304
00:17:52,570 --> 00:17:56,920
gets worse as you increase the
size of the language model.

305
00:17:56,920 --> 00:18:03,100
And then, I think, this was
13% will be not correlated.

306
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:06,710

307
00:18:06,710 --> 00:18:10,340
So you have something
like that, I don't know.

308
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:16,270
And then finally, a
pretty big portion, 33%,

309
00:18:16,270 --> 00:18:21,130
will be emergent abilities.

310
00:18:21,130 --> 00:18:27,880
And what I mean by that is
if you plot your compute

311
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:33,470
and accuracy for a certain
point, up to a certain point,

312
00:18:33,470 --> 00:18:37,160
your loss or your
accuracy will be 0,

313
00:18:37,160 --> 00:18:41,860
and then the accuracy
suddenly starts to improve.

314
00:18:41,860 --> 00:18:45,100
And so you can define
an emergent ability

315
00:18:45,100 --> 00:18:47,275
basically as for small models.

316
00:18:47,275 --> 00:18:51,530

317
00:18:51,530 --> 00:18:54,440
The performance is 0, so it's
not present in this model.

318
00:18:54,440 --> 00:19:03,460
And then for large models, you
have a much better than random

319
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:04,810
performance.

320
00:19:04,810 --> 00:19:06,430
And the interesting
thing about this

321
00:19:06,430 --> 00:19:09,520
is, let's say you had only
trained the small language

322
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:11,355
models up to that
point, you would

323
00:19:11,355 --> 00:19:12,730
have predicted
that it would have

324
00:19:12,730 --> 00:19:16,645
been impossible for the language
model to ever perform the task.

325
00:19:16,645 --> 00:19:18,520
But actually when you
train the larger model,

326
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:22,240
the language model does learn to
perform the task, so in a sense

327
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:24,170
it's pretty unpredictable.

328
00:19:24,170 --> 00:19:34,500

329
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:42,180
I'll talk about one final
thing, which is something called

330
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:45,510
inverse scaling/u-shaped
scaling.

331
00:19:45,510 --> 00:19:48,810

332
00:19:48,810 --> 00:19:52,510
OK, so I'll give a tricky
prompt to illustrate this.

333
00:19:52,510 --> 00:19:55,600
So the tricky prompt is this.

334
00:19:55,600 --> 00:20:02,090
Repeat after me.

335
00:20:02,090 --> 00:20:22,820
All that glisters is not glib,
all that glisters is not--

336
00:20:22,820 --> 00:20:25,970
and this is the prompt that
I give to the language model,

337
00:20:25,970 --> 00:20:28,850
and the goal is to
predict this next word.

338
00:20:28,850 --> 00:20:31,340
And obviously,
the correct answer

339
00:20:31,340 --> 00:20:37,100
is glib because you
asked to repeat after me.

340
00:20:37,100 --> 00:20:42,860
And what you see is, let's say
you have a extra small language

341
00:20:42,860 --> 00:20:47,810
model, a small language model
and a large language model.

342
00:20:47,810 --> 00:20:50,000
The performance for the
extra small language

343
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:55,370
model will be say here's 100%.

344
00:20:55,370 --> 00:20:59,090
The small language model is
actually worse at this task,

345
00:20:59,090 --> 00:21:00,980
so something like
something here.

346
00:21:00,980 --> 00:21:02,570
And then the large
language model

347
00:21:02,570 --> 00:21:06,140
again learns to do this task.

348
00:21:06,140 --> 00:21:09,140
So how do we basically
explain a behavior

349
00:21:09,140 --> 00:21:10,895
like this for a
prompt like this?

350
00:21:10,895 --> 00:21:21,310

351
00:21:21,310 --> 00:21:24,820
And the answer is,
you can decompose

352
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:30,310
this prompt into three subtasks
that are basically being done.

353
00:21:30,310 --> 00:21:36,160
So the first sub task is,
can you repeat some text?

354
00:21:36,160 --> 00:21:39,310
And if you draw the
plot here, again,

355
00:21:39,310 --> 00:21:44,440
extra small, small large,
and then here is 100.

356
00:21:44,440 --> 00:21:48,910
This is like a super easy task,
and so all the language models

357
00:21:48,910 --> 00:21:53,290
have perfect
performance on that.

358
00:21:53,290 --> 00:21:54,940
That's one hidden task.

359
00:21:54,940 --> 00:22:01,760
The other task is,
can you fix a quote?

360
00:22:01,760 --> 00:22:05,630
So the quote is supposed to be
all that glisters is not gold.

361
00:22:05,630 --> 00:22:12,200
And so you can then plot, again,
extra small, small, large.

362
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:14,450
What's the ability
to fix that quote?

363
00:22:14,450 --> 00:22:17,000
Well, the small language
model doesn't know the quote,

364
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,520
so it's going to get 0.

365
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:21,950
And then, the extra small
doesn't know the quote,

366
00:22:21,950 --> 00:22:23,240
so it's going to get 0.

367
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:27,440
The small will be able to do
it, and the large can obviously

368
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:31,700
do it, so that's what the
scaling curve looks like.

369
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:37,020
And then finally,
you have the quote--

370
00:22:37,020 --> 00:22:40,170
you have the task,
follow and instruction.

371
00:22:40,170 --> 00:22:43,390

372
00:22:43,390 --> 00:22:46,900
And obviously, this is
the instruction here.

373
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:49,840
And you can say, OK,
what's the performance

374
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:52,195
of these models on this task?

375
00:22:52,195 --> 00:22:56,700

376
00:22:56,700 --> 00:22:59,610
And the small model can't do
it, or the extra small model

377
00:22:59,610 --> 00:23:02,280
can't do it, small
model also can't do it,

378
00:23:02,280 --> 00:23:07,320
but the large model can do it,
so you get a curve like this.

379
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:10,980
And then why does this
explain this behavior here?

380
00:23:10,980 --> 00:23:16,500
Well, the small-- the extra
small model, it can repeat,

381
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:19,800
it can't fix the quote, and it
can't follow the instruction,

382
00:23:19,800 --> 00:23:23,281
so it actually gets it
correct, it says glib.

383
00:23:23,281 --> 00:23:26,190
It says glib.

384
00:23:26,190 --> 00:23:30,420
The small model can repeat.

385
00:23:30,420 --> 00:23:33,690
It can fix the quote, but it
doesn't follow the instruction,

386
00:23:33,690 --> 00:23:35,025
so it decides to fix the quote.

387
00:23:35,025 --> 00:23:39,910

388
00:23:39,910 --> 00:23:42,400
And then the large model,
it can do all three,

389
00:23:42,400 --> 00:23:44,830
it can follow the instruction,
so it just repeats.

390
00:23:44,830 --> 00:23:48,990

391
00:23:48,990 --> 00:23:52,620
And so that's how if you look
at the individual subtasks,

392
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:55,620
you can explain the behavior
of some of these weird scaling

393
00:23:55,620 --> 00:23:56,370
properties.

394
00:23:56,370 --> 00:23:59,530

395
00:23:59,530 --> 00:24:11,740
So I will conclude with one
general takeaway which is,

396
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:15,300
applicable if you do research.

397
00:24:15,300 --> 00:24:19,500
And the takeaway is to
just plot scaling curves.

398
00:24:19,500 --> 00:24:25,870

399
00:24:25,870 --> 00:24:28,370
And I'll just give a
really simple example.

400
00:24:28,370 --> 00:24:32,320
So let's say I do something
for my research project.

401
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:36,910
I fine tune a model on
some number of examples,

402
00:24:36,910 --> 00:24:42,450
and I get this is my thing.

403
00:24:42,450 --> 00:24:45,300
I get some performance there.

404
00:24:45,300 --> 00:24:51,370
And then here's the
line of not doing

405
00:24:51,370 --> 00:24:56,440
whatever my research project
is, and there's the performance.

406
00:24:56,440 --> 00:24:59,530
The reason you want to plot a
scaling curve for this is like,

407
00:24:59,530 --> 00:25:02,560
let's say you take
half the data,

408
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:06,280
and you find out that the
performance is actually here,

409
00:25:06,280 --> 00:25:09,780
so your curve looks like this.

410
00:25:09,780 --> 00:25:11,940
What this will tell
you is, you didn't

411
00:25:11,940 --> 00:25:14,010
have to collect all the
data to do your thing.

412
00:25:14,010 --> 00:25:16,470
And if you collect
more, you probably

413
00:25:16,470 --> 00:25:19,860
won't see an improvement
in performance.

414
00:25:19,860 --> 00:25:23,900
Another scenario would be
if you plotted that point

415
00:25:23,900 --> 00:25:27,780
and it was there, then your
curve will look like this.

416
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:30,990
And potentially,
if you kept doing

417
00:25:30,990 --> 00:25:33,480
more of whatever your
research project is,

418
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:36,860
you'd see an improvement
in performance.

419
00:25:36,860 --> 00:25:38,930
And then finally,
maybe your point

420
00:25:38,930 --> 00:25:43,120
is there, so your
curve looks like this.

421
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:45,330
And in this case
you would expect

422
00:25:45,330 --> 00:25:48,120
to see an even larger
jump in performance

423
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:51,240
after you continue
doing your thing.

424
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:55,020
So yeah, I'll end
my talk here, and

