560
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:42,103
will actually go shape
the future of AI,

561
00:31:42,103 --> 00:31:43,520
so maybe I should
talk about that.

562
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:45,410
It's a really important
goal and ambitious,

563
00:31:45,410 --> 00:31:47,060
and we really have
to get it right.

564
00:31:47,060 --> 00:31:49,940
So that could be a good
topic to think about.

565
00:31:49,940 --> 00:31:53,420
And when we talk about
something into the future,

566
00:31:53,420 --> 00:31:57,620
the best place to get an advice
is to look into the history.

567
00:31:57,620 --> 00:32:00,380
And in particular, we'll
look at the early history

568
00:32:00,380 --> 00:32:04,400
of transformer, and try to
learn many lessons from there.

569
00:32:04,400 --> 00:32:09,110
And the goal will be to
develop a unified perspective

570
00:32:09,110 --> 00:32:14,600
in which we can look into many
seemingly disjoint events.

571
00:32:14,600 --> 00:32:17,390
And from that we
can probably hope

572
00:32:17,390 --> 00:32:20,000
to project into the future
what might be coming.

573
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:24,448
And so that will be the
goal of this lecture,

574
00:32:24,448 --> 00:32:26,240
and we'll look at some
of the architectures

575
00:32:26,240 --> 00:32:29,940
of the transformers,
so let's get started.

576
00:32:29,940 --> 00:32:33,600
Everyone I see is
saying, AI is so

577
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:36,120
advancing so fast that
it's so hard to keep up.

578
00:32:36,120 --> 00:32:39,210
And it doesn't matter if you
have years of experience,

579
00:32:39,210 --> 00:32:41,610
there are so many things
are coming out every week

580
00:32:41,610 --> 00:32:43,200
that it's just hard to keep up.

581
00:32:43,200 --> 00:32:46,800
And I do see many people
spend a lot of time and energy

582
00:32:46,800 --> 00:32:50,340
catching up with this latest
developments, the cutting

583
00:32:50,340 --> 00:32:53,760
edge and the newest thing,
and then not enough attention

584
00:32:53,760 --> 00:32:58,620
goes into old things because
they become deprecated

585
00:32:58,620 --> 00:33:00,480
and no longer relevant.

586
00:33:00,480 --> 00:33:02,370
But I think it's
important, actually,

587
00:33:02,370 --> 00:33:05,340
to look into that because
we really need to--

588
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:08,070
when things are moving so
fast beyond our ability

589
00:33:08,070 --> 00:33:11,220
to catch up what we need to
do is study the change itself.

590
00:33:11,220 --> 00:33:14,370
And that means we can look
back at the previous things,

591
00:33:14,370 --> 00:33:16,530
and then look at
the current thing,

592
00:33:16,530 --> 00:33:19,650
and try to map how we got
here, and from which we

593
00:33:19,650 --> 00:33:22,800
can look into what where
we are heading towards.

594
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:27,970
So what does it mean to
study the change itself?

595
00:33:27,970 --> 00:33:31,840
First, we need to identify
the dominant driving

596
00:33:31,840 --> 00:33:33,490
forces behind the change.

597
00:33:33,490 --> 00:33:35,890
So here dominant is
an important word

598
00:33:35,890 --> 00:33:39,640
because typically, a change
has many, many driving forces,

599
00:33:39,640 --> 00:33:41,500
and we only care
about the dominant one

600
00:33:41,500 --> 00:33:43,510
because we're not trying
to get really accurate,

601
00:33:43,510 --> 00:33:46,000
we just want to have the
sense of directionality.

602
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:49,190
Second, we need to understand
the driving force really well,

603
00:33:49,190 --> 00:33:50,680
and then after
that we can predict

604
00:33:50,680 --> 00:33:55,360
the future trajectory by rolling
out the driving force and so on.

605
00:33:55,360 --> 00:33:57,160
And you heard that
right, I mentioned

606
00:33:57,160 --> 00:33:58,810
about predicting
the future, this

607
00:33:58,810 --> 00:34:01,690
is a computer science class not
like an astrology or something.

608
00:34:01,690 --> 00:34:02,800
But we do--

609
00:34:02,800 --> 00:34:06,670
I think it's actually not
that impossible to predict

610
00:34:06,670 --> 00:34:10,270
some future trajectory of a
very narrow scientific domain.

611
00:34:10,270 --> 00:34:13,480
And that endeavor
is really useful

612
00:34:13,480 --> 00:34:17,620
to do because let's
say you do all these

613
00:34:17,620 --> 00:34:21,610
and then make your prediction
accuracy to from 1% to 10%,

614
00:34:21,610 --> 00:34:24,340
and then you make say 100
predictions, 10 of them

615
00:34:24,340 --> 00:34:27,850
will be correct, say one of them
will be really, really correct.

616
00:34:27,850 --> 00:34:29,830
Meaning it will have
an outside impact

617
00:34:29,830 --> 00:34:31,389
that outweighs everything.

618
00:34:31,389 --> 00:34:35,170
And I think that is
kind of how many--

619
00:34:35,170 --> 00:34:37,540
I've seen many
general thing in life

620
00:34:37,540 --> 00:34:40,690
that you really have to
be right a few times.

621
00:34:40,690 --> 00:34:47,409
So why-- so if we think about
why predicting the future

622
00:34:47,409 --> 00:34:50,260
is difficult or maybe even
think about the extreme case

623
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:53,110
where we can all
do the prediction

624
00:34:53,110 --> 00:34:55,360
with perfect accuracy,
almost perfect accuracy.

625
00:34:55,360 --> 00:34:59,170
So here, I'm going to do a very
simple experiment of dropping

626
00:34:59,170 --> 00:35:04,030
this pen, and follow this
same three-step process.

627
00:35:04,030 --> 00:35:07,240
So we're going to identify
the dominant driving force.

628
00:35:07,240 --> 00:35:08,980
First of all, what
are the driving forces

629
00:35:08,980 --> 00:35:10,060
acting on this pen?

630
00:35:10,060 --> 00:35:12,370
Gravity downwards,
and is that all?

631
00:35:12,370 --> 00:35:16,360
We also have say
air friction, right?

632
00:35:16,360 --> 00:35:19,120
If I drop it, and that
will cause what's called

633
00:35:19,120 --> 00:35:21,190
a drag force acting upwards.

634
00:35:21,190 --> 00:35:26,190
And actually, depending on how
I drop this, the orientation,

635
00:35:26,190 --> 00:35:28,750
the aerodynamic interaction
will be so complicated

636
00:35:28,750 --> 00:35:32,650
that we don't currently have any
analytical way of modeling that.

637
00:35:32,650 --> 00:35:35,470
We can do it with the CFD, the
computational fluid dynamics,

638
00:35:35,470 --> 00:35:37,390
but it will be non-trivial.

639
00:35:37,390 --> 00:35:38,665
So we can neglect that.

640
00:35:38,665 --> 00:35:40,540
This is heavy enough
that gravity is probably

641
00:35:40,540 --> 00:35:44,050
the only dominant force,
so we simplify the problem.

642
00:35:44,050 --> 00:35:46,360
Second, do we understand
this dominant driving

643
00:35:46,360 --> 00:35:47,710
force, which is gravity?

644
00:35:47,710 --> 00:35:50,770
And we do because we have this
Newtonian mechanics, which

645
00:35:50,770 --> 00:35:52,300
provides a reasonably
good model.

646
00:35:52,300 --> 00:35:55,120
And then with that, we can
predict the future trajectory

647
00:35:55,120 --> 00:35:56,230
of this pen.

648
00:35:56,230 --> 00:35:59,770
And if we remember from
this dynamics class,

649
00:35:59,770 --> 00:36:02,470
if we have this
initial velocity is 0,

650
00:36:02,470 --> 00:36:04,060
I'm not going to
put any velocity.

651
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:06,130
And then let's say
position is 0 here,

652
00:36:06,130 --> 00:36:11,440
and then 1/2 GT squared will
give a precise trajectory

653
00:36:11,440 --> 00:36:13,390
of this pen as I drop this.

654
00:36:13,390 --> 00:36:16,930
So if there is a driving force,
single driving force that we

655
00:36:16,930 --> 00:36:19,960
really understand, it's
actually possible to predict

656
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:21,320
what's going to happen.

657
00:36:21,320 --> 00:36:26,470
So then, why do we really fear
about predicting the future

658
00:36:26,470 --> 00:36:27,790
in the most general sense?

659
00:36:27,790 --> 00:36:30,460
And I argue that,
among many reasons,

660
00:36:30,460 --> 00:36:33,970
the number of driving force, the
sheer number of dominant driving

661
00:36:33,970 --> 00:36:38,470
forces acting on the general
prediction is so complicated

662
00:36:38,470 --> 00:36:41,980
and their interaction creates
a complexity that we cannot

663
00:36:41,980 --> 00:36:43,540
predict the most general sense.

664
00:36:43,540 --> 00:36:45,280
So here is my cartoon
way of thinking

665
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:47,410
about the prediction of future.

666
00:36:47,410 --> 00:36:50,050
x-axis, we have a number
of dominant driving forces,

667
00:36:50,050 --> 00:36:52,120
y-axis we have a
prediction difficulty.

668
00:36:52,120 --> 00:36:54,400
So on the left hand
side, we have a dropping

669
00:36:54,400 --> 00:36:56,320
of pen it's a very simple case.

670
00:36:56,320 --> 00:36:57,820
It's a difficulty
it's very small,

671
00:36:57,820 --> 00:37:00,310
you just need to learn physics.

672
00:37:00,310 --> 00:37:04,960
And then, as you add more stuff,
it just becomes impossible.

673
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:08,050
So how does this fit
into the AI research?

674
00:37:08,050 --> 00:37:12,790
And you might think, OK, I see
all the time things are coming

675
00:37:12,790 --> 00:37:15,220
in, we are bombarded
by new things,

676
00:37:15,220 --> 00:37:17,320
and some people will come
up with the new agent,

677
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:20,080
new modality, new MMLU
score or whatever.

678
00:37:20,080 --> 00:37:22,810
We just see so
many things it just

679
00:37:22,810 --> 00:37:25,540
I'm not even able to catch
up with the latest thing,

680
00:37:25,540 --> 00:37:29,450
how can I even hope to predict
the future of the AI research?

681
00:37:29,450 --> 00:37:31,610
But I argue that
it's actually simpler

682
00:37:31,610 --> 00:37:34,460
because there is
a dominant driving

683
00:37:34,460 --> 00:37:36,890
force that is
governing a lot if any

684
00:37:36,890 --> 00:37:39,230
if not all of the AI research.

685
00:37:39,230 --> 00:37:41,510
And because of
that, I would like

686
00:37:41,510 --> 00:37:44,990
to point out that, it's
actually closer to the left

687
00:37:44,990 --> 00:37:48,920
than to the right than
we actually may perceive.

688
00:37:48,920 --> 00:37:52,560
So what is the driving force?

689
00:37:52,560 --> 00:37:54,660
Maybe before that, I
would like to caveat

690
00:37:54,660 --> 00:37:58,140
that when I do
this kind of talk,

691
00:37:58,140 --> 00:38:01,470
I would like to not focus too
much on the technical stuff

692
00:38:01,470 --> 00:38:04,740
which you can probably do
better in your own time,

693
00:38:04,740 --> 00:38:07,470
but rather I want to
share how I think.

694
00:38:07,470 --> 00:38:11,610
And for that, I want to
share how my opinion as is,

695
00:38:11,610 --> 00:38:14,280
and so it will be very
strongly opinionated.

696
00:38:14,280 --> 00:38:17,610
And by no means I'm saying
this is correct or not,

697
00:38:17,610 --> 00:38:19,590
I just wanted to
share my perspective.

698
00:38:19,590 --> 00:38:22,080
So coming back to this
driving force for AI,

699
00:38:22,080 --> 00:38:24,210
what is that dominant
driving force?

700
00:38:24,210 --> 00:38:26,970
And here is a plot
from Rich Sutton.

701
00:38:26,970 --> 00:38:31,410
And on the y-axis, we
have the calculations plot

702
00:38:31,410 --> 00:38:35,790
if you pay $100, and how much
computing power do you get?

703
00:38:35,790 --> 00:38:37,320
And it's in log scale.

704
00:38:37,320 --> 00:38:41,940
And then x-axis, we have a
time of more than 100 years.

705
00:38:41,940 --> 00:38:44,820
So this is actually
more than exponential.

706
00:38:44,820 --> 00:38:50,310
And I don't know any trend that
is as strong and as long lasting

707
00:38:50,310 --> 00:38:51,190
as this one.

708
00:38:51,190 --> 00:38:55,520
So whenever I see this kind
of thing, I should say,

709
00:38:55,520 --> 00:38:57,430
OK, I should not
compete with this.

710
00:38:57,430 --> 00:39:01,480
And better, I should try to
leverage as much as possible.

711
00:39:01,480 --> 00:39:07,810
And so what this means is, you
get 10x more compute every five

712
00:39:07,810 --> 00:39:10,240
years if you spend the
same amount of dollar.

713
00:39:10,240 --> 00:39:14,710
And so in other words, you
get the cost of compute

714
00:39:14,710 --> 00:39:16,270
is going down exponentially.

715
00:39:16,270 --> 00:39:19,960
And this associated
scaling is really

716
00:39:19,960 --> 00:39:24,160
dominating the AI research, and
that is somewhat hard to take,

717
00:39:24,160 --> 00:39:27,710
but that is, I think, really
important to think about.

718
00:39:27,710 --> 00:39:30,460
So coming back to
this AI research,

719
00:39:30,460 --> 00:39:32,500
how is this
exponentially cheaper

720
00:39:32,500 --> 00:39:35,260
compute drive the AI research?

721
00:39:35,260 --> 00:39:37,570
Let's think about the job
of the AI researchers.

722
00:39:37,570 --> 00:39:41,140
It is to teach machines how to
think in a very general sense.

723
00:39:41,140 --> 00:39:45,340
And one somewhat
unfortunately common approach

724
00:39:45,340 --> 00:39:50,710
is, we think about how we teach
machine how we think we think.

725
00:39:50,710 --> 00:39:54,910
So meaning, we
model how we think

726
00:39:54,910 --> 00:39:57,340
and then try to incorporate
that into some kind

727
00:39:57,340 --> 00:39:59,230
of mathematical
model and teach that.

728
00:39:59,230 --> 00:40:01,870
And now, the question
is, do we understand how

729
00:40:01,870 --> 00:40:03,880
we think at the very low level?

730
00:40:03,880 --> 00:40:05,290
I don't think we do.

731
00:40:05,290 --> 00:40:06,760
I have no idea what's going on.

732
00:40:06,760 --> 00:40:08,950
So it's fundamentally
flawed in the sense

733
00:40:08,950 --> 00:40:11,650
that we try to model something
that we have no idea about.

734
00:40:11,650 --> 00:40:14,590
And what happens if we go
with this kind of approach

735
00:40:14,590 --> 00:40:16,720
is that, it poses
a structure that

736
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:18,790
serves as a shortcut
in the short term,

737
00:40:18,790 --> 00:40:21,370
and so you can maybe get
a paper or something.

738
00:40:21,370 --> 00:40:23,860
But then it becomes
a bottleneck when--

739
00:40:23,860 --> 00:40:28,870
because we don't know how this
will limit further scaling up.

740
00:40:28,870 --> 00:40:31,000
More fundamentally
what this is doing

741
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:33,670
is, we are limiting
the degree of freedom

742
00:40:33,670 --> 00:40:36,040
we are giving to the
machines, and that

743
00:40:36,040 --> 00:40:37,510
will backfire at some point.

744
00:40:37,510 --> 00:40:42,010
And this has been
going on for decades.

745
00:40:42,010 --> 00:40:47,050
And bitter lesson is, I think
the single most important piece

746
00:40:47,050 --> 00:40:48,490
of writing in AI.

747
00:40:48,490 --> 00:40:51,460
And it says, this is
my wording by the way.

748
00:40:51,460 --> 00:40:54,190
Say past 70 years of
entire AI research

749
00:40:54,190 --> 00:40:58,060
can be summarized into
developing progressively more

750
00:40:58,060 --> 00:41:00,370
general method with
weaker modeling

751
00:41:00,370 --> 00:41:03,160
assumptions or inductive
biases, and add more data

752
00:41:03,160 --> 00:41:04,720
and compute, in
other words scale up.

753
00:41:04,720 --> 00:41:09,370
And that has been the recipe
of entire AI research, not

754
00:41:09,370 --> 00:41:10,390
fancy things.

755
00:41:10,390 --> 00:41:14,470
And if you think about
this, the models of 2000

756
00:41:14,470 --> 00:41:17,770
is a lot more difficult
than what we use now.

757
00:41:17,770 --> 00:41:21,010
And so it's much easier
to get into AI nowadays

758
00:41:21,010 --> 00:41:22,600
from technical perspective.

759
00:41:22,600 --> 00:41:27,700
So this is, I think, the
really the key information.

760
00:41:27,700 --> 00:41:30,880
We have this compute cost
is going down exponentially,

761
00:41:30,880 --> 00:41:34,120
and it's getting cheaper faster
than we are becoming a better

762
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:34,720
researcher.

763
00:41:34,720 --> 00:41:37,660
So don't compete with that and
just try to leverage that as

764
00:41:37,660 --> 00:41:40,810
much as possible, and
that is the driving force

765
00:41:40,810 --> 00:41:43,450
that I wanted to identify.

766
00:41:43,450 --> 00:41:46,265
And I'm not saying this
is the only driving force,

767
00:41:46,265 --> 00:41:47,890
but this is the
dominant driving force,

768
00:41:47,890 --> 00:41:49,930
so we can probably
neglect the other ones.

769
00:41:49,930 --> 00:41:52,030
So here's a graphical
version of that.

770
00:41:52,030 --> 00:41:54,190
x-axis we have a
compute, y-axis we have

771
00:41:54,190 --> 00:41:55,390
a performance of some kind.

772
00:41:55,390 --> 00:41:57,700
Let's think about some
general intelligence.

773
00:41:57,700 --> 00:42:00,370
And let's look at two
different methods.

774
00:42:00,370 --> 00:42:03,280
One, with more structure,
more modeling assumptions,

775
00:42:03,280 --> 00:42:04,578
fancier math, whatever.

776
00:42:04,578 --> 00:42:06,370
And then the other one
is a less structure.

777
00:42:06,370 --> 00:42:10,750
What you see is typically, you
start with a better performance

778
00:42:10,750 --> 00:42:12,550
when you have a
low compute regime,

779
00:42:12,550 --> 00:42:14,800
and then but it plateaus
because of some kind

780
00:42:14,800 --> 00:42:16,158
of structure backfiring.

781
00:42:16,158 --> 00:42:17,950
And then with the less
structure because we

782
00:42:17,950 --> 00:42:19,690
give a lot more
freedom to the model,

783
00:42:19,690 --> 00:42:21,370
it doesn't work
in the beginning.

784
00:42:21,370 --> 00:42:23,860
But then as we add more
compute, it starts working,

785
00:42:23,860 --> 00:42:28,370
and then it gets better, we
call this more scalable method.

786
00:42:28,370 --> 00:42:32,080
So does that mean we should
just go with the least

787
00:42:32,080 --> 00:42:35,530
structure, most freedom
to the model possible way

788
00:42:35,530 --> 00:42:36,400
from the get go?

789
00:42:36,400 --> 00:42:38,080
And the answer
is, obviously, no.

790
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:40,270
Let's think about even
less structured case.

791
00:42:40,270 --> 00:42:44,500
This red one here is, it
will pick up a lot later

792
00:42:44,500 --> 00:42:46,660
and requires a lot more compute.

793
00:42:46,660 --> 00:42:49,600
So it really depends
on where we are.

794
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:52,930
We cannot indefinitely wait
for the most general case.

795
00:42:52,930 --> 00:42:54,940
And so let's think
about the case

796
00:42:54,940 --> 00:42:57,790
where our compute situation
is at this dotted line.

797
00:42:57,790 --> 00:43:01,060
If we're here, we should
choose this last structure

798
00:43:01,060 --> 00:43:03,822
one as opposed to this
even less structure one

799
00:43:03,822 --> 00:43:05,530
because the other one
doesn't really work

800
00:43:05,530 --> 00:43:07,000
and the other one works.

801
00:43:07,000 --> 00:43:08,800
But crucially, we
need to remember

802
00:43:08,800 --> 00:43:11,290
that we are adding some
structure because we

803
00:43:11,290 --> 00:43:13,990
don't have compute, so we
need to remove that later.

804
00:43:13,990 --> 00:43:16,690
And so the difference
between these two methods

805
00:43:16,690 --> 00:43:20,320
is that additional inductive
biases or structure we impose,

806
00:43:20,320 --> 00:43:23,980
somewhat impose that
typically don't get removed.

807
00:43:23,980 --> 00:43:26,080
So adding this.

808
00:43:26,080 --> 00:43:28,150
What that means is
that, at the given

809
00:43:28,150 --> 00:43:31,570
level of compute data
algorithmic development

810
00:43:31,570 --> 00:43:33,820
and architecture
that we have, there's

811
00:43:33,820 --> 00:43:36,280
like an optimal inductive
bias or structure

812
00:43:36,280 --> 00:43:39,520
that we can add to the
problem to make the progress.

813
00:43:39,520 --> 00:43:42,910
And that has been really how
we have made so much progress.

814
00:43:42,910 --> 00:43:45,610
But these are like shortcuts
that hinder further scaling

815
00:43:45,610 --> 00:43:47,500
later on, so we
have to remove them

816
00:43:47,500 --> 00:43:50,420
later on when we have more
compute, better algorithm,

817
00:43:50,420 --> 00:43:51,750
or whatever.

818
00:43:51,750 --> 00:43:55,640
And as a community, we do
adding structure very well

819
00:43:55,640 --> 00:43:59,120
and because there's an incentive
structure with like papers.

820
00:43:59,120 --> 00:44:01,520
You add a nice one
then you get a paper.

821
00:44:01,520 --> 00:44:04,310
But removing that doesn't
really get you much,

822
00:44:04,310 --> 00:44:06,260
so that we don't really do that.

823
00:44:06,260 --> 00:44:08,570
And I think we should
do a lot more of those.

824
00:44:08,570 --> 00:44:11,900
So maybe another implication
of this bitter lesson

825
00:44:11,900 --> 00:44:15,920
is that, because of this what
is better in the long-term

826
00:44:15,920 --> 00:44:19,070
almost necessarily
looks worse now.

827
00:44:19,070 --> 00:44:21,740
And this is quite
unique to AI research

828
00:44:21,740 --> 00:44:25,580
because the AI research
of current paradigm

829
00:44:25,580 --> 00:44:27,020
is learning based method.

830
00:44:27,020 --> 00:44:30,590
Meaning that we are
giving models freedom,

831
00:44:30,590 --> 00:44:32,630
the machines choose
how they learn.

832
00:44:32,630 --> 00:44:36,530
So because we need to give
more freedom, it gives--

833
00:44:36,530 --> 00:44:39,890
it's a more chaotic at the
beginning, so it doesn't work.

834
00:44:39,890 --> 00:44:41,900
But then when it
started working,

835
00:44:41,900 --> 00:44:44,810
we can put in more compute
and then it can be better,

836
00:44:44,810 --> 00:44:47,860
so it's really important
to have this in mind.

837
00:44:47,860 --> 00:44:52,060
So to summarize, we have
identified this dominant driving

838
00:44:52,060 --> 00:44:54,580
force behind the AI
research, and that

839
00:44:54,580 --> 00:44:56,590
is this exponentially
cheaper compute,

840
00:44:56,590 --> 00:44:59,800
and associated scaling up.

841
00:44:59,800 --> 00:45:02,140
Now, that we have identified
if you remember back

842
00:45:02,140 --> 00:45:05,020
from my initial slides,
we-- the next step

843
00:45:05,020 --> 00:45:07,570
is to understand this
driving force better.

844
00:45:07,570 --> 00:45:11,830
And so that we're going to spend
most of the time doing that.

845
00:45:11,830 --> 00:45:16,175
And for that, we need to go back
to some history of transformer

846
00:45:16,175 --> 00:45:17,675
because this is a
transformers class

847
00:45:17,675 --> 00:45:21,070
and analyze key
structures and decisions

848
00:45:21,070 --> 00:45:23,740
that were made by the
researchers at the time,

849
00:45:23,740 --> 00:45:25,120
and why they did that.

850
00:45:25,120 --> 00:45:27,250
Whether that was an
optimal structure that

851
00:45:27,250 --> 00:45:28,960
could have been
added at the time,

852
00:45:28,960 --> 00:45:33,435
and why they might be irrelevant
now, and should we remove that?

853
00:45:33,435 --> 00:45:35,560
And we'll go through some
of the practices of this.

854
00:45:35,560 --> 00:45:37,600
And hopefully,
this will give you

855
00:45:37,600 --> 00:45:42,020
some flavor of what scaling
research looks like.

856
00:45:42,020 --> 00:45:45,610
So now, we'll go into a little
bit of the technical stuff, tech

857
00:45:45,610 --> 00:45:48,550
transformer architecture,
there are some variants.

858
00:45:48,550 --> 00:45:50,620
I'll talk about three of them.

859
00:45:50,620 --> 00:45:52,450
First, is the
encoder-decoder, which

860
00:45:52,450 --> 00:45:54,640
is the original transformer,
which has a little bit

861
00:45:54,640 --> 00:45:55,390
more structure.

862
00:45:55,390 --> 00:45:59,710
Second one is the encoder-only,
which is popularized by Bert.

863
00:45:59,710 --> 00:46:02,500
And then third one
is decoder-only,

864
00:46:02,500 --> 00:46:05,770
which you can think of it
as a current like GPT-3

865
00:46:05,770 --> 00:46:08,740
or other language models,
this has a lot less structure

866
00:46:08,740 --> 00:46:09,820
than the encoder-decoder.

867
00:46:09,820 --> 00:46:12,820
So these are the three
types we'll go into detail.

868
00:46:12,820 --> 00:46:15,610
Second, the encoder-only
is actually not

869
00:46:15,610 --> 00:46:17,650
that useful in the
most general sense.

870
00:46:17,650 --> 00:46:21,370
It still has some place,
but we will just briefly

871
00:46:21,370 --> 00:46:22,870
go over that, and
then spend most

872
00:46:22,870 --> 00:46:24,910
of the time comparing 1 and 3.

873
00:46:24,910 --> 00:46:28,090
So one has more structure,
what's the implication of that

874
00:46:28,090 --> 00:46:29,630
and so on?

875
00:46:29,630 --> 00:46:32,470
So first of all, let's think
about what a transformer

876
00:46:32,470 --> 00:46:35,620
is, just at a very high level.

877
00:46:35,620 --> 00:46:37,630
Or first principles,
what is a transformer?

878
00:46:37,630 --> 00:46:38,680
Is a sequence model.

879
00:46:38,680 --> 00:46:42,190
And sequence model is has
an input of a sequence.

880
00:46:42,190 --> 00:46:44,950
So sequence can be--
a sequence of elements

881
00:46:44,950 --> 00:46:47,680
can be words, or
images, or whatever.

882
00:46:47,680 --> 00:46:49,000
It's a very general concept.

883
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:51,760
In this particular example
I show you with the words,

884
00:46:51,760 --> 00:46:53,530
sentence is a sequence of words.

885
00:46:53,530 --> 00:46:55,900
And then we the first
step is to tokenize it

886
00:46:55,900 --> 00:47:00,730
because we have to represent
this word in computers which

887
00:47:00,730 --> 00:47:04,210
requires some kind
of encoding scheme,

888
00:47:04,210 --> 00:47:07,420
so we just do it with a
fixed number of integers,

889
00:47:07,420 --> 00:47:09,730
and then we have now a
sequence of integers.

890
00:47:09,730 --> 00:47:12,760
And then the dominant
paradigm nowadays

891
00:47:12,760 --> 00:47:14,950
is to represent each
sequence element

892
00:47:14,950 --> 00:47:16,930
as a vector, dense
vector, because we

893
00:47:16,930 --> 00:47:18,530
know how to multiply them well.

894
00:47:18,530 --> 00:47:21,100
And then so we have a
sequence of vectors.

895
00:47:21,100 --> 00:47:25,540
And finally, this sequence
model will do the following.

896
00:47:25,540 --> 00:47:29,050
We just want to model the
interaction between sequence

897
00:47:29,050 --> 00:47:29,740
elements.

898
00:47:29,740 --> 00:47:33,940
And we do that by let them take
the dot product of each other.

899
00:47:33,940 --> 00:47:35,680
And if the dot
product is high, we

900
00:47:35,680 --> 00:47:37,540
can say semantically
they are more related

901
00:47:37,540 --> 00:47:39,550
than the dot
products that is low,

902
00:47:39,550 --> 00:47:41,440
and that's kind of
the sequence model.

903
00:47:41,440 --> 00:47:44,410
And the transformer is a
particular type of sequence

904
00:47:44,410 --> 00:47:49,510
model that uses what's called
attention to model this

905
00:47:49,510 --> 00:47:50,380
interaction .

906
00:47:50,380 --> 00:47:54,430
So let's get into the details
of this encoder-decoder, which

907
00:47:54,430 --> 00:47:55,720
was the original transformer.

908
00:47:55,720 --> 00:47:57,070
It's quite many, many pieces.

909
00:47:57,070 --> 00:48:00,320
So let's go into a little
bit a piece at a time.

910
00:48:00,320 --> 00:48:01,780
So starting with the encoder.

911
00:48:01,780 --> 00:48:03,790
So here I'm going to
show you an example

912
00:48:03,790 --> 00:48:07,930
of machine translation, which
used to be very cool thing.

913
00:48:07,930 --> 00:48:11,410
And so you are-- you have an
English sentence that is good,

914
00:48:11,410 --> 00:48:13,480
and then we're going to
translate it into German.

915
00:48:13,480 --> 00:48:17,630
So first thing is to encode
this into a dense vector.

916
00:48:17,630 --> 00:48:21,310
So here I'm representing
it with this a vector

917
00:48:21,310 --> 00:48:22,660
of size three or something.

918
00:48:22,660 --> 00:48:25,150
And then we have to let
them take the dot product.

919
00:48:25,150 --> 00:48:29,170
So this lines
represent which element

920
00:48:29,170 --> 00:48:32,300
can talk to which
element, other elements.

921
00:48:32,300 --> 00:48:34,190
And here because
it's an input, we

922
00:48:34,190 --> 00:48:36,350
take what is called a
bidirectional attention,

923
00:48:36,350 --> 00:48:38,390
so any token can talk
to any other token.

924
00:48:38,390 --> 00:48:41,030
And then we have this
MLP or feedforward

925
00:48:41,030 --> 00:48:44,240
layer which is per token, it
doesn't have any interaction,

926
00:48:44,240 --> 00:48:49,190
we just do some multiplication
just because we can do it.

927
00:48:49,190 --> 00:48:53,210
And then that's one layer,
and we repeat that n times,

928
00:48:53,210 --> 00:48:55,490
and that's just the
transformer encoder.

929
00:48:55,490 --> 00:49:00,290
And at the end, what you get
is the sequence of vectors each

930
00:49:00,290 --> 00:49:04,340
representing the sequence
element in this case a word.

931
00:49:04,340 --> 00:49:06,350
So that's the output
of this encoder.

932
00:49:06,350 --> 00:49:08,240
Now, let's look at
the decoder, which

933
00:49:08,240 --> 00:49:11,210
is similarly shaped
stack of layers.

934
00:49:11,210 --> 00:49:16,910
So here we put in as an input
what the answer should be.

935
00:49:16,910 --> 00:49:19,178
So here, BOS, is the
beginning of sequence,

936
00:49:19,178 --> 00:49:21,470
and then das ist gut, I don't
know how to pronounce it,

937
00:49:21,470 --> 00:49:23,750
but that's the German
translation of, that is good.

938
00:49:23,750 --> 00:49:26,750
And so we kind of go
through the similar process.

939
00:49:26,750 --> 00:49:28,820
Here we have a causal
self-attention,

940
00:49:28,820 --> 00:49:33,500
meaning that the tokens of time
step t can only attend to t

941
00:49:33,500 --> 00:49:36,110
and before because when
we start generating it,

942
00:49:36,110 --> 00:49:39,050
we don't have the future
tokens, so we cannot--

943
00:49:39,050 --> 00:49:42,270
when we train it, we should
limit that, and that way,

944
00:49:42,270 --> 00:49:45,290
this is done by like
masking, but this

945
00:49:45,290 --> 00:49:47,300
is different from the encoder.

946
00:49:47,300 --> 00:49:50,120
So after this, you can get--

947
00:49:50,120 --> 00:49:54,830
after again, n layers, you
get this sequence output,

948
00:49:54,830 --> 00:49:57,565
and you have this--
the output of sequence,

949
00:49:57,565 --> 00:49:58,940
to sequence, to
sequence mapping,

950
00:49:58,940 --> 00:50:01,520
this is a general
encoder-decoder architecture.

951
00:50:01,520 --> 00:50:03,590
And when you get
this end of sequence,

952
00:50:03,590 --> 00:50:04,760
you stop generating it.

953
00:50:04,760 --> 00:50:07,070
So this is the overall picture.

954
00:50:07,070 --> 00:50:10,130
Now, I'll point out some
important attention patterns.

955
00:50:10,130 --> 00:50:14,360
So we are translating
into German what

956
00:50:14,360 --> 00:50:15,860
is into input to the encoder.

957
00:50:15,860 --> 00:50:18,410
So there has to be some
connection between the decoder

958
00:50:18,410 --> 00:50:20,930
and the encoder that is
done by this cross attention

959
00:50:20,930 --> 00:50:25,130
mechanism shown in this red,
which is just that each vector

960
00:50:25,130 --> 00:50:28,220
representation of each
sequence in the output decoder

961
00:50:28,220 --> 00:50:31,370
should attend to some of them in
the encoder, and that is done.

962
00:50:31,370 --> 00:50:34,880
In particular the design
feature which is interesting

963
00:50:34,880 --> 00:50:37,580
is that all the
layers in the decoder

964
00:50:37,580 --> 00:50:40,670
attend to the final layer
output of the encoder,

965
00:50:40,670 --> 00:50:43,940
and we'll come back to the
implication of this design.

966
00:50:43,940 --> 00:50:46,400
So yeah, that's that.

967
00:50:46,400 --> 00:50:49,610
And now, move on to the
second type of architecture,

968
00:50:49,610 --> 00:50:50,630
which is encoder-only.

969
00:50:50,630 --> 00:50:52,213
We'll spend a little
bit of time here.

970
00:50:52,213 --> 00:50:54,110
So again, we have to--

971
00:50:54,110 --> 00:50:59,150
we have the same input, and we
go through a similar structure.

972
00:50:59,150 --> 00:51:02,990
And then in this case, the
final output is a single vector.

973
00:51:02,990 --> 00:51:05,270
Regardless of the
length of the sequence,

974
00:51:05,270 --> 00:51:06,890
we just get a single vector.

975
00:51:06,890 --> 00:51:11,060
And that is that represent
the input sequence, that's

976
00:51:11,060 --> 00:51:12,920
a dense vector representation.

977
00:51:12,920 --> 00:51:16,070
And then let's say we do some
kind of a sentiment analysis,

978
00:51:16,070 --> 00:51:18,440
we run through a task
specific linear layer

979
00:51:18,440 --> 00:51:20,630
to map it to
classification label

980
00:51:20,630 --> 00:51:22,910
positive or negative
probabilities here.

981
00:51:22,910 --> 00:51:28,100
And that's required for all
these task specific cases.

982
00:51:28,100 --> 00:51:30,020
And this is kind of
popularized by Bert.

983
00:51:30,020 --> 00:51:34,010
And what this means is
that, here at the time,

984
00:51:34,010 --> 00:51:38,390
2018, when Bert came out we
had the benchmark called glue,

985
00:51:38,390 --> 00:51:40,220
which was a language
understanding task.

986
00:51:40,220 --> 00:51:42,320
You have a sequence in
classification labels

987
00:51:42,320 --> 00:51:43,400
out for most cases.

988
00:51:43,400 --> 00:51:46,750
This was how the field
really advanced at the time.

989
00:51:46,750 --> 00:51:48,960
So when we care
about such tasks,

990
00:51:48,960 --> 00:51:50,970
then there's an
incentive to think

991
00:51:50,970 --> 00:51:52,620
about simplifying
the problem, adding

992
00:51:52,620 --> 00:51:55,120
the structure to the problem
so that we can make a progress.

993
00:51:55,120 --> 00:51:56,640
So this, the
additional structure

994
00:51:56,640 --> 00:51:58,830
that was put into this
particular architecture

995
00:51:58,830 --> 00:52:02,400
is that we're going to
give up on the generation.

996
00:52:02,400 --> 00:52:05,460
If we do that, it becomes
a lot simpler problem.

997
00:52:05,460 --> 00:52:08,100
Instead of sequence to sequence,
we're talking about sequence

998
00:52:08,100 --> 00:52:11,460
to classification labels, and
that's just so much easier.

999
00:52:11,460 --> 00:52:16,560
And so at some point, 2018,
2019, a lot of the papers

1000
00:52:16,560 --> 00:52:18,900
are just research was
like, we sometimes

1001
00:52:18,900 --> 00:52:21,510
call it Bert engineers, a
little bit change of something

1002
00:52:21,510 --> 00:52:24,720
get like 0.5% better
on glue, and you get

1003
00:52:24,720 --> 00:52:26,020
a paper, and things like that.

1004
00:52:26,020 --> 00:52:29,370
It was very chaotic error, and--

1005
00:52:29,370 --> 00:52:31,710
but if we look at
from this perspective,

1006
00:52:31,710 --> 00:52:35,100
we are putting the sequence
structure of not generating

1007
00:52:35,100 --> 00:52:38,700
the sequence that puts a
lot of performance win,

1008
00:52:38,700 --> 00:52:41,170
but in the long term,
it's not really useful.

1009
00:52:41,170 --> 00:52:44,010
So we're not going to look at
this encoder-only architecture

1010
00:52:44,010 --> 00:52:44,780
going forward.

1011
00:52:44,780 --> 00:52:46,850
Third architecture,
decoder-only.

1012
00:52:46,850 --> 00:52:49,430
This one is my
favorite personally.

1013
00:52:49,430 --> 00:52:53,510
And it looks kind of daunting
because of this attention

1014
00:52:53,510 --> 00:52:56,300
pattern, but it
actually is very simple.

1015
00:52:56,300 --> 00:52:59,300
So here, we only
have a single stack,

1016
00:52:59,300 --> 00:53:01,400
and it can actually
generate stuff.

1017
00:53:01,400 --> 00:53:05,480
And so there's misconception
that some people think this

1018
00:53:05,480 --> 00:53:08,120
decoder-only architecture is
used for language modeling next

1019
00:53:08,120 --> 00:53:10,790
to prediction, so it cannot be
used for supervised learning.

1020
00:53:10,790 --> 00:53:12,140
But here, we can actually do it.

1021
00:53:12,140 --> 00:53:15,170
The trick is to have
this input that is good,

1022
00:53:15,170 --> 00:53:16,790
concatenate it with the target.

1023
00:53:16,790 --> 00:53:19,490
And if you do that,
then it just becomes

1024
00:53:19,490 --> 00:53:21,440
simple to sequence
in sequence out.

1025
00:53:21,440 --> 00:53:24,650
So what we do is, the
self-attention mechanism here

1026
00:53:24,650 --> 00:53:27,950
is actually handling
both the cross attention

1027
00:53:27,950 --> 00:53:31,220
between target and the input,
and self-attention sequence

1028
00:53:31,220 --> 00:53:32,780
learning within each.

1029
00:53:32,780 --> 00:53:34,490
So that's the causal attention.

1030
00:53:34,490 --> 00:53:38,540
And then as I mentioned,
the output is a sequence,

1031
00:53:38,540 --> 00:53:40,790
and then the key
design features are

1032
00:53:40,790 --> 00:53:43,010
self-attention is
serving both roles,

1033
00:53:43,010 --> 00:53:47,040
and we are in some sense sharing
the parameters between input

1034
00:53:47,040 --> 00:53:47,580
and target.

1035
00:53:47,580 --> 00:53:50,490
So same set of parameters
are applied to both input

1036
00:53:50,490 --> 00:53:51,690
and the target sequences.

1037
00:53:51,690 --> 00:53:54,330
So this is the
decoder only, now,

1038
00:53:54,330 --> 00:53:56,220
we will go into the comparison.

1039
00:53:56,220 --> 00:53:58,950
So I think there are many--

1040
00:53:58,950 --> 00:54:01,410
like they look very different,
at least, on the schematics.

1041
00:54:01,410 --> 00:54:03,390
So how different
are they actually?

1042
00:54:03,390 --> 00:54:07,770
And I argue that they're
actually quite similar.

1043
00:54:07,770 --> 00:54:09,960
And so to illustrate
that we're going

1044
00:54:09,960 --> 00:54:12,600
to transform starting from
this encoder-decoder which

1045
00:54:12,600 --> 00:54:14,610
has more structures
built in, and then

1046
00:54:14,610 --> 00:54:17,160
into the decoder-only
architecture

1047
00:54:17,160 --> 00:54:19,740
and see what are some
of the differences.

1048
00:54:19,740 --> 00:54:21,720
And then interpret
those differences,

1049
00:54:21,720 --> 00:54:24,660
those additional structures,
are they relevant nowadays now

1050
00:54:24,660 --> 00:54:28,030
that we have more compute
better algorithm and so on?

1051
00:54:28,030 --> 00:54:30,990
So let's have this
table, four differences

1052
00:54:30,990 --> 00:54:32,400
we'll see each of them.

1053
00:54:32,400 --> 00:54:36,240
And then as we go through,
we will populate this table.

1054
00:54:36,240 --> 00:54:39,390
So let's first look at this
additional cross-attention.

1055
00:54:39,390 --> 00:54:42,030
What that means is
that, this on the left

1056
00:54:42,030 --> 00:54:44,670
is an encoder-decoder, which
has this additional red block,

1057
00:54:44,670 --> 00:54:46,635
the cross-attention
compared to the simpler

1058
00:54:46,635 --> 00:54:47,760
one that doesn't have that.

1059
00:54:47,760 --> 00:54:51,570
So we want to make it, make
the left closer to the right,

1060
00:54:51,570 --> 00:54:55,050
so that means we need to either
get rid of it or something.

1061
00:54:55,050 --> 00:54:59,400
And attention mechanism has kind
of the four projection matrices,

1062
00:54:59,400 --> 00:55:01,882
and so self-attention and
cross attention actually

1063
00:55:01,882 --> 00:55:03,840
have the same number of
parameters, same shape,

1064
00:55:03,840 --> 00:55:04,980
so we can just share them.

1065
00:55:04,980 --> 00:55:07,170
So that's the first step,
share both of these,

1066
00:55:07,170 --> 00:55:09,780
and then it becomes
mostly the same mechanism.

1067
00:55:09,780 --> 00:55:12,240
And then so that's
the first difference

1068
00:55:12,240 --> 00:55:14,610
separate cross-attention
or self-attention

1069
00:55:14,610 --> 00:55:15,600
serving both roles.

1070
00:55:15,600 --> 00:55:17,730
Second difference is
the parameter sharing.

1071
00:55:17,730 --> 00:55:21,210
So what that means is
that, between the input

1072
00:55:21,210 --> 00:55:23,580
and the target,
encoder-decoder architecture

1073
00:55:23,580 --> 00:55:24,900
uses a separate parameters.

1074
00:55:24,900 --> 00:55:27,850
And decoder-only only
has a single stack

1075
00:55:27,850 --> 00:55:29,530
so it uses the shared parameter.

1076
00:55:29,530 --> 00:55:32,110
So if we want to make
the left close to right,

1077
00:55:32,110 --> 00:55:35,110
we want to share the encoder
parameters, so let's do that.

1078
00:55:35,110 --> 00:55:38,260
I just color this, so now,
they share the parameters.

1079
00:55:38,260 --> 00:55:41,590
Third difference is the target
to input attention pattern.

1080
00:55:41,590 --> 00:55:44,110
So we need to connect
the target to the input.

1081
00:55:44,110 --> 00:55:46,060
And how does that--
how is it done?

1082
00:55:46,060 --> 00:55:49,090
In the encoder-decoder case,
we had this cross attention.

1083
00:55:49,090 --> 00:55:53,530
And then in the decoder-only,
it's a per the self-attention

1084
00:55:53,530 --> 00:55:54,370
doing everything.

1085
00:55:54,370 --> 00:55:56,980
What the difference
is that we have

1086
00:55:56,980 --> 00:56:02,020
this every layer of the decoder
attending to the final layer

1087
00:56:02,020 --> 00:56:05,350
output of the encoder, whereas
if you think about this decoder

1088
00:56:05,350 --> 00:56:07,510
is actually per
layer, within layer

1089
00:56:07,510 --> 00:56:11,140
when we are decoding
the say word thus,

1090
00:56:11,140 --> 00:56:15,460
we are looking at the same layer
representation of the encoder,

1091
00:56:15,460 --> 00:56:17,650
and that's within
layer, and I think

1092
00:56:17,650 --> 00:56:18,940
this is the design feature.

1093
00:56:18,940 --> 00:56:20,770
So if we want to make
this close to that,

1094
00:56:20,770 --> 00:56:24,250
we have to bring back this
attention to each layer.

1095
00:56:24,250 --> 00:56:28,090
So now, layer 1 will be
attending to layer 1 of this.

1096
00:56:28,090 --> 00:56:32,140
And that's then finally,
the last difference

1097
00:56:32,140 --> 00:56:33,250
is the input attention.

1098
00:56:33,250 --> 00:56:35,320
I mentioned about this
bidirectional attention.

1099
00:56:35,320 --> 00:56:38,770
And because we have this
decoder only typically,

1100
00:56:38,770 --> 00:56:41,110
with the unidirectional
attention,

1101
00:56:41,110 --> 00:56:43,660
we need to make them
matching, so that's

1102
00:56:43,660 --> 00:56:45,100
the we can just get rid of it.

1103
00:56:45,100 --> 00:56:47,650
I just got rid of the
some of the arrows.

1104
00:56:47,650 --> 00:56:51,250
So then at this point,
these two architectures

1105
00:56:51,250 --> 00:56:52,857
are almost identical.

1106
00:56:52,857 --> 00:56:54,940
A little bit of difference
in the cross attention,

1107
00:56:54,940 --> 00:56:56,830
but same number of parameters.

1108
00:56:56,830 --> 00:56:58,940
And if you have
in deep learning,

1109
00:56:58,940 --> 00:57:01,050
if you just train these,
these two architecture

1110
00:57:01,050 --> 00:57:02,810
in the same task,
same data, I think,

1111
00:57:02,810 --> 00:57:04,935
you will get pretty much
within the noise, probably

1112
00:57:04,935 --> 00:57:06,920
closer than if you train
the same thing twice.

1113
00:57:06,920 --> 00:57:09,830
So I would say
they are identical.

1114
00:57:09,830 --> 00:57:11,900
And so these are the
main differences.

1115
00:57:11,900 --> 00:57:16,220
Now, we'll look at what are
the additional structures, what

1116
00:57:16,220 --> 00:57:18,150
they mean, it means.

1117
00:57:18,150 --> 00:57:19,310
So yeah.

1118
00:57:19,310 --> 00:57:21,950
That's the populated
table now, and then

1119
00:57:21,950 --> 00:57:24,590
so we can say that
encoder-decoder compared

1120
00:57:24,590 --> 00:57:29,510
to the decoder-only architecture
has these additional structures

1121
00:57:29,510 --> 00:57:32,990
inductive biases built in, so
let's go into each of them.

1122
00:57:32,990 --> 00:57:37,400
The first one is what
encoder-decoder tries added

1123
00:57:37,400 --> 00:57:40,130
as a structure is that, input
and the target sequences are

1124
00:57:40,130 --> 00:57:42,290
sufficiently different that we--

1125
00:57:42,290 --> 00:57:45,140
it will be useful to use a
separate parameters, that's

1126
00:57:45,140 --> 00:57:46,040
the assumption.

1127
00:57:46,040 --> 00:57:49,040
And so why is that a useful?

1128
00:57:49,040 --> 00:57:51,110
When can that
assumption be useful?

1129
00:57:51,110 --> 00:57:54,110
And one example is
machine translation.

1130
00:57:54,110 --> 00:57:56,690
Back when the transformer
was introduced in 2017,

1131
00:57:56,690 --> 00:57:58,940
translation was
really popular task,

1132
00:57:58,940 --> 00:58:00,800
and it was difficult,
considered difficult.

1133
00:58:00,800 --> 00:58:03,650
And because it's a
sequence-to-sequence

1134
00:58:03,650 --> 00:58:05,660
and you can actually
have a blue score which

1135
00:58:05,660 --> 00:58:07,520
is heuristic based
method that can give you

1136
00:58:07,520 --> 00:58:10,320
a single number and then
people can optimize that.

1137
00:58:10,320 --> 00:58:13,700
So what that in
that task, we have

1138
00:58:13,700 --> 00:58:16,280
this input and target in
completely different languages.

1139
00:58:16,280 --> 00:58:19,070
So if the goal is to
learn translation only,

1140
00:58:19,070 --> 00:58:20,870
then it kind of
makes sense to have,

1141
00:58:20,870 --> 00:58:22,490
OK, this parameter
in the encoder

1142
00:58:22,490 --> 00:58:24,980
will take care of the
English, and this parameter

1143
00:58:24,980 --> 00:58:28,190
in the decoder will take care of
the German, that seems natural.

1144
00:58:28,190 --> 00:58:30,470
And what about now?

1145
00:58:30,470 --> 00:58:33,530
Modern language models is
about learning knowledge.

1146
00:58:33,530 --> 00:58:35,540
And it's not just
about translation

1147
00:58:35,540 --> 00:58:37,250
or not even about
language, languages

1148
00:58:37,250 --> 00:58:41,000
comes up as a byproduct of
doing this next token prediction

1149
00:58:41,000 --> 00:58:42,900
and translation as well.

1150
00:58:42,900 --> 00:58:45,950
So does it make sense to
have a separate parameter

1151
00:58:45,950 --> 00:58:48,890
for this kind of situation now?

1152
00:58:48,890 --> 00:58:52,100
We have some
knowledge in German,

1153
00:58:52,100 --> 00:58:54,320
some knowledge in English,
and we-- if anything

1154
00:58:54,320 --> 00:58:55,340
we want to combine them.

1155
00:58:55,340 --> 00:58:58,070
And if we represent them
in a separate parameters,

1156
00:58:58,070 --> 00:58:59,510
I don't think that's natural.

1157
00:58:59,510 --> 00:59:03,950
So I would say with this much
more general, larger models that

1158
00:59:03,950 --> 00:59:07,010
can do a lot of
things, this assumption

1159
00:59:07,010 --> 00:59:09,020
seems very unnatural to me.

1160
00:59:09,020 --> 00:59:11,810
Second example is a
little bit more modern.

1161
00:59:11,810 --> 00:59:14,900
Two years ago when I was
at Google and with Jason,

1162
00:59:14,900 --> 00:59:17,660
we did this instruction
fine tuning work.

1163
00:59:17,660 --> 00:59:21,440
And what this is, is you take
the pre-trained model, and then

1164
00:59:21,440 --> 00:59:24,350
just fine tune on
academic data set,

1165
00:59:24,350 --> 00:59:26,540
and so that it can understand
the natural language

1166
00:59:26,540 --> 00:59:27,200
instruction.

1167
00:59:27,200 --> 00:59:29,630
So the detail doesn't
matter, but here

1168
00:59:29,630 --> 00:59:32,600
let's think about
the performance gain

1169
00:59:32,600 --> 00:59:35,760
by doing this fine tuning on
two different architectures we

1170
00:59:35,760 --> 00:59:36,260
tried.

1171
00:59:36,260 --> 00:59:39,200
So first five is
the Flan-T5, which

1172
00:59:39,200 --> 00:59:41,900
is T5 base which is the
encoder-decoder architecture.

1173
00:59:41,900 --> 00:59:45,440
Last one, the latter five
decoder-only architecture

1174
00:59:45,440 --> 00:59:46,340
based on PaLM.

1175
00:59:46,340 --> 00:59:52,400
So we spent 99% of the time on
PaLM, optimizing a lot of these,

1176
00:59:52,400 --> 00:59:55,100
and then at the end we just
spent like three days on T5.

1177
00:59:55,100 --> 00:59:58,320
But the performance gain
was a lot higher on this.

1178
00:59:58,320 --> 01:00:01,670
And I was really confused about
this, and in a very good way.

1179
01:00:01,670 --> 01:00:03,440
And after the paper
was published,

1180
01:00:03,440 --> 01:00:05,962
I wanted to take a little
bit deeper into why

1181
01:00:05,962 --> 01:00:06,920
this might be the case.

1182
01:00:06,920 --> 01:00:12,200
So my hypothesis is that,
it's about the length.

1183
01:00:12,200 --> 01:00:16,430
So academic data sets we
use, we use like 1,832 tasks.

1184
01:00:16,430 --> 01:00:20,450
And here they have this very
distinctive characteristic

1185
01:00:20,450 --> 01:00:23,180
where we have a long
input, long in order

1186
01:00:23,180 --> 01:00:24,650
to make the task more difficult.

1187
01:00:24,650 --> 01:00:27,530
But then we cannot make the
target long because if we do,

1188
01:00:27,530 --> 01:00:31,070
there's no way to create it, so
there's fundamental challenge

1189
01:00:31,070 --> 01:00:31,650
of that.

1190
01:00:31,650 --> 01:00:34,250
So what happens is, you
have a long text of input

1191
01:00:34,250 --> 01:00:36,590
and then short
text of the target.

1192
01:00:36,590 --> 01:00:39,860
And so this is kind of the
length distribution of what it

1193
01:00:39,860 --> 01:00:41,990
went into the Flan fine tuning.

1194
01:00:41,990 --> 01:00:47,240
So then you see this, you
have a very different sequence

1195
01:00:47,240 --> 01:00:49,217
going into the
encoder as an input,

1196
01:00:49,217 --> 01:00:51,800
and the very different type of
sequence going into the target.

1197
01:00:51,800 --> 01:00:54,745
So now, this
encoder-decoder architecture

1198
01:00:54,745 --> 01:00:56,870
has an assumption that they
will be very different.

1199
01:00:56,870 --> 01:01:00,280
That structure really
shines because of this.

1200
01:01:00,280 --> 01:01:03,700
It was a kind of an accident,
but that was, I think,

1201
01:01:03,700 --> 01:01:08,650
why this really architecture was
just suitable for fine tuning

1202
01:01:08,650 --> 01:01:10,240
with the academic data sets.

1203
01:01:10,240 --> 01:01:11,470
What about now?

1204
01:01:11,470 --> 01:01:14,110
Do we care about this
kind of assumption?

1205
01:01:14,110 --> 01:01:17,170
And if you think about the
general use cases of language

1206
01:01:17,170 --> 01:01:20,920
models nowadays, if anything
the more interesting cases

1207
01:01:20,920 --> 01:01:24,310
involve longer
generation, longer target.

1208
01:01:24,310 --> 01:01:26,680
Just because we
cannot grade them,

1209
01:01:26,680 --> 01:01:28,852
doesn't mean that we are
not interested in them.

1210
01:01:28,852 --> 01:01:31,060
Actually, if anything we
are more interested in that.

1211
01:01:31,060 --> 01:01:34,060
So now we have this
longer target situation,

1212
01:01:34,060 --> 01:01:37,390
so this separate sequence
length parameter doesn't

1213
01:01:37,390 --> 01:01:38,830
seem to make much sense.

1214
01:01:38,830 --> 01:01:41,710
And moreover, we
think about this chat

1215
01:01:41,710 --> 01:01:45,160
application like ChatGPT, we
do multi-turn conversation.

1216
01:01:45,160 --> 01:01:48,100
And then so what is
a target of this turn

1217
01:01:48,100 --> 01:01:49,960
becomes the input
of the next turn.

1218
01:01:49,960 --> 01:01:52,000
And then my question
is, does that

1219
01:01:52,000 --> 01:01:56,110
make sense to even think
about a different parameters

1220
01:01:56,110 --> 01:01:59,800
if next turn is going
to be the same thing?

1221
01:01:59,800 --> 01:02:04,060
So that was the first inductive
bias we just mentioned.

1222
01:02:04,060 --> 01:02:07,210
And then the second structure
is that, target element can only

1223
01:02:07,210 --> 01:02:10,660
attend to the fully encoded
ones, the final output

1224
01:02:10,660 --> 01:02:11,610
of the encoder.

1225
01:02:11,610 --> 01:02:14,150
Let's look at this additional
structure, what that means.

1226
01:02:14,150 --> 01:02:19,670
So as I mentioned, we have this
very top layer attending to it.

1227
01:02:19,670 --> 01:02:23,080
And so in deep neural
nets, typically, we

1228
01:02:23,080 --> 01:02:25,150
see that the bottom
layers and the top layers

1229
01:02:25,150 --> 01:02:28,090
encode information at
a very different level.

1230
01:02:28,090 --> 01:02:31,300
Meaning that, for example, in
computer vision, lower layer--

1231
01:02:31,300 --> 01:02:32,860
the bottom layers
encode something

1232
01:02:32,860 --> 01:02:36,730
like edges, top layers, higher
levels combining the features,

1233
01:02:36,730 --> 01:02:38,080
something like cat face.

1234
01:02:38,080 --> 01:02:39,700
And so we call
this deep learning

1235
01:02:39,700 --> 01:02:43,180
a hierarchical representation
learning method.

1236
01:02:43,180 --> 01:02:48,130
And so now the question is,
if decoder layer one attends

